---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Mermaid from '../../../components/Mermaid.astro';
import { seriesParts, partHref } from '../../../data/series';
---

<Base title="Project CB — MyAir Control Bridge">
	<p><a href="/">← back to home</a></p>

	<h1>Project CB — MyAir Control Bridge</h1>
	<div style="margin: 0.75rem 0 1.5rem">
		<span class="badge live">● live since 2026-08-12</span>
		<span class="badge">rust</span>
		<span class="badge">rs-485</span>
		<span class="badge">websocket</span>
		<span class="badge">android</span>
		<span class="badge">reverse engineering</span>
	</div>

	<p class="tagline">
		How I replaced the vendor control path of my ducted air conditioning — a wall tablet
		running repackaged vendor apps, a Rust daemon speaking reverse-engineered RS-485, and a
		Pi Zero W in a tupperware container in the ceiling — without losing a single feature,
		and with instant rollback the whole way.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the final state</h2>
	<Mermaid>{`flowchart LR
		CB["Advantage Air Control Box<br/>(ceiling)"]
		PI["Pi Zero W — airconcb<br/>cb-daemon (Rust)"]
		TAB["Wall tablet — Galaxy Tab A 8.0<br/>myair5 + aaservice2"]
		HOME["Home automations"]

		CB <-->|"RS-485 half-duplex<br/>57600 8N1"| PI
		PI <-->|"WebSocket :2026<br/>JSON mailbox stream"| TAB
		TAB <-->|"HTTP :2025<br/>(vendor API)"| HOME`}</Mermaid>

	<p>
		Every register read and write between the vendor UI and the control box now travels
		through hardware and software I control. The vendor apps still run — repackaged to
		drop their signing requirements — so the wall panel looks and behaves exactly as it
		always did.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the series</h2>
	<ul class="series-list">
		{seriesParts.map((p) => (
			<li>
				<span class="series-n">part {p.n}</span>
				<a href={partHref(p)}>{p.title}</a>
				<span class="series-blurb">{p.blurb}</span>
			</li>
		))}
	</ul>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>repositories</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<a href="https://github.com/retsimx/cb-daemon">retsimx/cb-daemon</a> — the Rust
			register-mailbox sync daemon (AOA/TTY → WebSocket mailbox API)
		</li>
		<li>
			<a href="https://github.com/retsimx/aaservice">retsimx/aaservice</a> — the Kotlin
			reimplementation of the vendor AA Service app, dual-transport (USB | WebSocket)
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>standing on the shoulders of</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			My own reverse engineering of the vendor APK and OEM hardware came first; the
			community effort <a href="https://github.com/gundy/aa_interop">gundy/aa_interop</a> documented the CB↔tablet RS-485 protocol independently, and I've since contributed
			corrections and improvements back to it
		</li>
		<li>
			<a href="https://blog.hopefullyuseful.com/blog/advantage-air-ezone-tablet-diy-repair/"
				>“Air Con: $1697 for an on/off switch”</a
			> — the OEM-tablet DIY repair write-up that proves how common this failure mode is
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>status</h2>
	<div class="status-line">
		<span><span class="dot"></span>bridge: live in production</span>
		<span><span class="dot"></span>vendor UI: fully functional</span>
		<span><span class="dot amber"></span>write-up: in progress</span>
	</div>
</Base>
